The most effective process parameters for influencing material removal rate (MRR) depend on the material and study, but several passages identify peak current and pulse/on times as dominant factors in EDM performance related to MRR.

- S1#c1 (Nowicki et al. 2022, Hastelloy C-22 with graphite electrodes): The discharge current and pulse duration are the main factors determining MRR (and Ra) for both graphite electrode grades tested. {{cite:S1#c1}}
- S2#c1 (Nas & Kara 2022, corrosion-resistant superalloy with SCT/DCT): Peak current is the most effective factor for MRR, with ANOVA showing peak current at 74.79% influence on Ra and 86.43% on MRR. Pulse-on time also matters in the Taguchi-gray relational analysis. {{cite:S2#c1}}
- S3#c1 (Nowicki et al. 2024, Hastelloy C-22 with fine vs. coarse graphite grain): Graphite grain size and machining parameters impact MRR; the study develops models linking EDM parameters to material removal effectiveness, with fine-grained graphite associated with higher process efficiency (MRR) under negative polarity. {{cite:S3#c1}}
- S8#c1 (Akıncıoğlu 2022, TiGr2 alloy): Amperage (current) is the most effective factor for MRR, with ANOVA attributing the highest influence to current. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Synthesis: Across materials, current-related parameters (discharge/current and pulse/on time) repeatedly emerge as primary drivers of MRR, with peak current singled out as most influential in at least one study (S2#c1). There is some variation by material and electrode/grain choice (S1#c1 emphasizes discharge current and pulse duration; S3#c1 highlights electrode grain size and polarity). What they do not cover in detail here is a direct, cross-material comparison of the same parameter set. Your library does not contain enough on this. Try adding sources on: comparative EDM parameter effects across alloys.

