Corrosion behavior of SLM maraging steel is improved by certain heat treatments and by reversed austenite formation, with several studies showing enhanced pitting resistance and wear-corrosion performance after specific thermochemical processing, but results depend on the prior heat treatment and resulting microstructure. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}}

- S4#c1 (Khan et al. 2022) reports that in 3.5 wt% NaCl, 720°C-tempered maraging steel (with reversed austenite) exhibits higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, or homogenized specimens; reversed austenite nucleates along martensite lath boundaries due to Ni-enrichment, enhancing pitting resistance. {{cite:S4#c1}}

- S2#c1 (Godec et al. 2021) finds that plasma nitriding of 18Ni-300 AM maraging steel, when coupled with prior aging, improves wear and corrosion resistance; nitriding without prior aging creates cracks, while aging plus nitriding reduces defects and improves protective layer performance. {{cite:S2#c1}}

- S3#c1 (Tan et al. 2018) characterizes as-fabricated and heat-treated SLM 300 maraging steel, showing heat treatments relieve residual stress and can affect corrosion resistance, with comprehensive corrosion performance discussed alongside microstructure and precipitates. {{cite:S3#c1}}

- S6#c1 (Haghdadi et al. 2020) notes AM steels, including maraging/PH steels, can exhibit enhanced corrosion resistance due to unique AM microstructures, though ductility and fatigue remain challenging; the review links in situ transformations to corrosion behavior. {{cite:S6#c1}}

- S8#c1 (Ahmadkhaniha et al. 2021) reports that electrochemically, nano-hardness and corrosion potential in SLM maraging steel after heat treatment are similar to wrought maraging steel; aging increases hardness but reduces corrosion potential, and precipitation governs hardness and corrosion; electropolishing reduces roughness. {{cite:S8#c1}}

