- Ahmadkhaniha et al. (2021) find that SLM produces a fine-grained martensitic microstructure with relatively rough surfaces; in heat-treated conditions, nano-hardness and corrosion behavior are similar to conventional wrought maraging steel, with aging yielding the highest hardness and the lowest corrosion potential. Precipitation drives both hardness and corrosion behavior. Electropolishing reduces surface roughness but does not dramatically change the overall comparison to wrought steel. {{cite:S8#c1}} 

- Tan et al. (2018) report that heat treatments influence corrosion resistance in as-fabricated and heat-treated SLM maraging steels, with comprehensive corrosion performance data discussed alongside other properties. {{cite:S3#c1}} 

- Khan et al. (2022) show that reversed austenite formation during intercritical heat treatment can improve pitting resistance, evidenced by higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate in tempered specimens compared to as-printed or aged without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

- Godec et al. (2021) indicate nitriding of additive-manufactured maraging steel enhances wear and corrosion resistance when prior aging is applied, while nitriding after aging can introduce cracks; overall, nitriding with prior aging improves corrosion resistance versus conventional counterparts. {{cite:S2#c1}} 

- Haghdadi et al. (2020) conclude AM, including maraging/PH steels, can yield microstructures with features that enhance corrosion resistance compared with conventional steels, though ductility and fatigue remain challenges. {{cite:S6#c1}}

