Yes, some diabetes apps can reduce HbA1c, but effects are mixed across studies.

- Agarwal et al. 2018 (BlueStar, multicenter pragmatic RCT): no significant HbA1c reduction at 3 months versus control (mean difference −0.42, 95% CI −1.05 to 0.21; P=.19). However, exploratory analysis suggested each additional day of app use associated with a 0.016-point HbA1c decrease, and site-level usage varied widely. {{cite:S6#c1}}
- Cafazzo et al. 2012 (adolescents with type 1 diabetes, not type 2): reported improved frequency of blood glucose monitoring with gamification; authors note extending to HbA1c improvements requires a properly powered study, implying no proven HbA1c reduction in this study. {{cite:S2#c1}}
- Zhao et al. 2016 (broad health apps): reviewed across various apps and behaviors; many studies showed behavior change and some impact on outcomes, but the review calls for larger, high-quality trials, without giving a quantified HbA1c effect for type 2 diabetes apps specifically. {{cite:S4#c1}}
- Davies et al. 2022 (consensus/guidelines): not reporting HbA1c effect sizes for apps; focuses on recommendations for broader diabetes management and cardiorenal protection. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Overall: some apps show potential for HbA1c reduction, but robust, clinically meaningful effects are not consistently demonstrated across studies; when reductions are observed, they are small and may depend on usage level and context. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

