People stop using diabetes apps for a mix of barriers and declines in perceived value. Studies identify barriers to adoption and, importantly, barriers to continued use, including lack of awareness, usability issues, and misalignment with users’ needs. Facilitators and motivators include information and personalized guidance, tracking for awareness and progress, credibility, goal setting, reminders, and sharing.

- Barriers to continued use include lack of knowledge about apps, perceived disease severity, and limitations in technical or health literacy. {{cite:S5#c1}}
- Design features that encourage ongoing use involve tracking, reminders, personalized guidance, credibility, and sharing capabilities, which can sustain engagement. {{cite:S5#c1}}
- User perceptions and experiences indicate that user-centered design, ease of use, and integration with routine care influence whether individuals continue using apps. {{cite:S8#c1}}
- Qualitative work shows that respondents value visual trends, intuitive navigation, and convenience, while barriers include awareness and relevance to daily self-management. {{cite:S8#c1}}

